  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nA complex weather system will impact eastern New York and\nwestern New England with multiple rounds of showers and\nthunderstorms tomorrow through Wednesday. Initially, warm air\nadvection showers will spread into the region tomorrow morning\ninto early tomorrow afternoon beneath an upper-level shortwave\npulsing through a parent trough whose axis will still be\nsituated upstream through the Great Lakes and Ohio River Valley.\nMost of these initial showers should be light, though increased\ndiffluence aloft could help to make for some locally moderate\nrates. Then, in the early to mid-late afternoon, as we get into\nthe convergent zone of the anticyclonically curved jet aloft, \nwe'll get a brief break before another round of showers and\npotential thunderstorms track through the region. \n\nThe Storm Prediction Center has maintained the Marginal (Level 1\nof 5) to Slight (Level 2 of 5) Risk for severe weather tomorrow\nfor this next round of showers and storms given the level of \ndeep- layer shear within the environment. However, confidence is\nvery low in the overall severe threat for tomorrow given\nnumerous factors: 1) extensive cloud cover, 2) a \ncontaminated/stabilized environment given the early showers, 3) \ndelayed timing of the incoming warm front that will limit our \ntime in the true warm sector and, subsequently, our ability to \ntruly destabilize, and 4) a decent capping inversion that may\nkeep all storms elevated in nature. That said, if the front is a\nlittle quicker than what the latest guidance suggests, we could\nhave some stronger storms that produce locally damaging wind\ngusts.\n\nThe main threat particularly with this second round of showers\nand storms, and any additional rounds that occur overnight, is\nheavy rainfall that could result in isolated flash flooding.\nOur FFG is about 2\" to 2.5\" in three hours, which seems high,\nbut could be attainable given the potential for training showers\nand storms. If the same areas get hit multiple times with\nrainfall, this could lead to more than just nuisance issues.\nThis is particularly true late tomorrow afternoon into the\nevening. PWATs are seen increasing to nearly 2\" or greater\nacross much of the area. Strong forcing from the parent trough\naloft and a surface wave that will precede the cold front \ntomorrow night will drive efficient precipitation processes\nwhich could lead to 1\" per hour rates even just briefly. Latest\nensembles show a low probability of this, but given the signals\nfor potential training and the fairly wet antecedent \nconditions, an isolated flash flood cannot be ruled out \nparticularly in the Mid-Hudson Valley and southwest New \nEngland.\n\n\nShowers will continue through tomorrow night before gradually\ndecreasing in spatial spread throughout the day Wednesday.\nBroad cyclonic flow will force showers to become limited to\nhigher terrain areas by Wednesday afternoon before dry\nconditions return regionwide. At this time, QPF ranges from \nabout 0.5\" to 1.25\" with locally higher amounts of 1.5\" \npossible. These amounts could be superseded, however, given the\naforementioned outcomes.",
